# Write numbers, measurements, dates and time

Write numbers, units, dates and times in one plain style, so people can read them quickly and without mistakes. The examples use made-up homes, rooms and devices.

<h2 id="designing-for-people-who-struggle-with-numbers">Design content for people who struggle with numbers</h2>

Many adults find numbers hard. Some people have dyscalculia, a lasting difficulty with understanding numbers.

People who struggle with numbers may have problems with:

- entering numbers, such as a pairing code, a setup code or a PIN
- reference numbers, and not knowing what to do with them, such as an error code in a notification
- telling the number 0 from the letter O, for example in a setup code or an entity ID
- numbers in tables and charts, such as the figures in an energy dashboard table

Put numbers into context in sentences. Say what the number means and what to do about it.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Error 0x1F4 | The hub could not connect to the network. Check the router is on, then try again. |
| Pairing code: 4081 | To add the doorbell, enter pairing code 4081 in the app within 5 minutes. |

If you are thinking about visual aids to explain statistics or comparisons, be cautious. There is not enough research to recommend them yet. Test any visual aid with the people who will use it.

<h2 id="numbers">Numbers</h2>

<h3 id="numerals">Numerals (1, 2, 3 and so on)</h3>

Use numerals for numbers, including 1 and 2, for example for statistics, time, measurements, lists, points or steps. People find numerals easier to read, and they scan for them. See the [A to Z entry on numbers](../style-guides/a-to-z.html#numbers).

For numbers over 999, use a comma, for example 1,000.

Avoid decimal points. Round to the nearest whole number, except for:

- money
- readings where the decision needs the decimal, such as energy, tariffs and temperature to one decimal place

If you need a number less than 1, put 0 before the decimal point, for example 0.25. Charts and data owns the rule on precision. Read [Keep meaningful precision](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-071).

Examples:

- Wait 1 to 2 minutes before you press the button again.
- The hub takes 1 to 3 minutes to restart.
- The garage door was open for 2 hours.

<h3 id="numerals-at-the-start-of-a-sentence">Use numerals at the start of a sentence</h3>

You can start a sentence with a numeral. If it looks confusing, reword the sentence.

Example:

12 sensors are online and 2 are offline.

<h3 id="numerals-in-headings">Use numerals in headings</h3>

You can use numerals in headings.

Examples:

- 5 ways to cut standby energy use
- 4-in-1 multisensor

<h3 id="numerals-in-lists">Use numerals in lists</h3>

You can use numerals in lists.

Example:

The starter kit includes:

- 1 hub
- 2 motion sensors
- 4 AAA batteries

<h3 id="where-we-do-not-use-numerals">Where not to use numerals</h3>

<h4 id="one">One</h4>

Spell out “one” when it means “a”, or to avoid repeating a word.

Example:

Do not set 2 heating schedules for the same room. Delete the old one.

Also use “one” in phrases such as:

- “one or the other”
- “one of the most common”
- “one at a time”

<h4 id="two-sets-of-numerals">Two sets of numerals</h4>

Some amounts are clearer in words than in numerals. See [Amounts and units](#amounts-and-units).

<h2 id="ordinal-numbers">Ordinal numbers (1st, 2nd, 3rd and so on)</h2>

Use numerals with letter suffixes for ordinal numbers, including 1st and 2nd, for example for quantities and things in a list or sequence. For dates, write the day as a number, as [Dates](#dates) shows.

Make the letters the same size as the numbers. Do not use raised letters (superscript), because screen readers do not always read them out correctly and they can confuse people.

Examples:

- the 2nd reminder
- the 3rd attempt to connect
- the 5th day of the schedule

Do not use ordinal numbers in phrases such as:

- first aid
- first time
- first visit
- secondhand
- second opinion

## Measurements

<h3 id="energy">Energy</h3>

Use “energy” when you talk about energy in general, for example “energy use on the dashboard”. Where it helps, explain that energy is measured in kilowatt-hours, written “kWh”, the first time you use it.

Use “kWh” as the unit. Do not put a space between the number and the unit.

Example:

The oven used 2.4kWh today.

Power and energy are different. Read [kilowatts and kilowatt-hours](../style-guides/a-to-z.html#kilowatts-and-kilowatt-hours) and [Keep units with their values](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-073).

<h3 id="amounts-and-units">Amounts and units</h3>

Do not use a space between the amount and the unit.

Example:

The bulbs use 40W to 60W each.

Use your judgement when you explain amounts. Avoid having two sets of numerals next to each other. Sometimes the clearest way to write an amount is in words, because numerals would confuse.

| We say | We do not say |
| --- | --- |
| Fit two 40W bulbs. | Fit 2 40W bulbs. |

<h3 id="temperature">Temperature</h3>

Use Celsius, and write the symbol straight after the number, for example 21°C. Use Fahrenheit only if the household chose it. See [Celsius](../style-guides/a-to-z.html#celsius).

Give the reading, its unit and the time it was taken, so people know how current it is. Read [Say which time the data covers](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-074).

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| It is warm in the nursery. | Nursery temperature at 7:30pm: 21°C. |

<h3 id="metric-and-imperial">Metric and imperial</h3>

Use metric. If it helps, add imperial in brackets, for example in advice on fitting a cable: “Cut the cable to 3m (about 10ft).”

Do not add imperial to a reading that Home Assistant shows, or to a value someone must enter exactly. Show the household’s own unit choice, as the [A to Z entry on Celsius](../style-guides/a-to-z.html#celsius) says.

<h2 id="dates">Dates</h2>

Write the day, the month in full, then the year: 6 August 2026. When the day of the week helps, for example when booking or scheduling, write Thursday 6 August 2026.

Spell out months in full as far as possible. Screen readers read shortened months, such as Jan and Feb, in inconsistent and sometimes confusing ways.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Service due 2nd Jun | Boiler service on Tuesday 2 June 2026 |

See [Respect number and date preferences](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-072) and the [A to Z entry on dates](../style-guides/a-to-z.html#dates).

<h2 id="time">Time</h2>

Civica respects the household’s clock setting, 12 hour or 24 hour, as [Respect number and date preferences](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-072) says. Use one style on a screen. The table shows the 12-hour style.

| Use | Do not use |
| --- | --- |
| 5pm | 5.00pm or 1700hrs |
| 5:30pm | 5.30pm or 1730hrs |
| midnight | 12am |
| midday | 12 noon, 12pm |
| 6 hours 30 minutes | 6.5hrs |

“Midnight” can mean the start or the end of a day. Read [Make ambiguous times explicit](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-075). See also the [A to Z entry on times](../style-guides/a-to-z.html#times).

<h2 id="ranges">Ranges of numbers, dates and time</h2>

Use “to” for time, date and number ranges, not hyphens or dashes.

Examples:

- The heating runs Monday to Friday, 6am to 8am.
- The sensor read 18°C to 21°C overnight.

<h2 id="fractions-and-percentages">Fractions and percentages</h2>

Spell out common fractions, such as two-thirds.

Example:

The hot water tank is two-thirds full.

For percentages, use the % symbol with no space, for example 50%. It is often better not to use a percentage. Instead of 50%, you could say “1 in 2” or “half”. Keep the % symbol for a reading that Home Assistant shows as a percentage, such as a battery level. See the [A to Z entry on per cent](../style-guides/a-to-z.html#per-cent).

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| The back door was left open on 50% of days this week. | The back door was left open on half of the days this week. |

<h2 id="statistics">Statistics</h2>

Use statistics sparingly. Think about who is reading and what they need to decide.

Use the clearest, most meaningful format for what you are showing. For example, use “1 in 7 days” rather than “14.3% of days”.

Avoid mixing number formats. Use one or the other.

Example:

The back door was left open for more than 10 minutes on around 7 in every 10 days in March and around 5 in every 10 days in April.

For plain wording around numbers, read [Use clear language](clear-language.html).
